WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.953 --> 00:00:02.453
Welcome to LocalFlow.

2
00:00:02.453 --> 00:00:08.251
In this tutorial, we'll turn a short story idea into a complete shot list, ready for

3
00:00:08.251 --> 00:00:10.115
image and video generation.

4
00:00:11.152 --> 00:00:13.177
Everything lives in a project.

5
00:00:13.177 --> 00:00:18.486
Click New project, give it a name, and choose an aspect ratio and frame rate for the

6
00:00:18.486 --> 00:00:18.802
film.

7
00:00:20.788 --> 00:00:24.463
The project opens on the Story tab, which works in four steps.

8
00:00:24.463 --> 00:00:26.563
Step one is the idea.

9
00:00:26.563 --> 00:00:28.488
A few sentences are enough.

10
00:00:28.807 --> 00:00:33.787
You can also steer the result with a genre, a tone, a target length in seconds, and

11
00:00:33.787 --> 00:00:36.007
style notes for the look of the film.

12
00:00:36.581 --> 00:00:37.594
Click Refine.

13
00:00:37.594 --> 00:00:44.230
Your language model, from OpenAI, Anthropic, or OpenRouter, rewrites the idea as a

14
00:00:44.230 --> 00:00:48.681
filmable story with a clear beginning, middle, and end.

15
00:00:54.623 --> 00:00:56.198
Here's the refined story.

16
00:00:56.198 --> 00:00:57.723
You can edit it right here.

17
00:00:57.723 --> 00:01:02.709
Every change is saved as a revision, so you can compare versions and restore an

18
00:01:02.709 --> 00:01:04.098
older one at any time.

19
00:01:05.898 --> 00:01:08.311
Step three is the story bible.

20
00:01:08.311 --> 00:01:14.423
It describes the visual style, and every character, location, and prop in the story.

21
00:01:20.372 --> 00:01:22.822
Each part of the bible becomes an ingredient.

22
00:01:22.822 --> 00:01:27.971
Ingredients keep characters and places consistent from shot to shot, and you can

23
00:01:27.971 --> 00:01:31.897
lock any of them, so a rebuild never overwrites your changes.

24
00:01:33.873 --> 00:01:36.736
Finally, break the story down into shots.

25
00:01:36.736 --> 00:01:41.964
LocalFlow sizes every shot to the clip length of your video model, and writes a

26
00:01:41.964 --> 00:01:47.061
keyframe prompt, a video prompt, camera direction, and dialogue for each one.

27
00:01:55.437 --> 00:01:56.675
Open the Shots board.

28
00:01:56.675 --> 00:02:00.412
Each scene is a row, and each shot is a card.

29
00:02:00.412 --> 00:02:04.825
Drag the cards to reorder them, or to move a shot to another scene.

30
00:02:05.388 --> 00:02:07.526
Click a shot to open its details.

31
00:02:07.526 --> 00:02:12.994
You can edit every prompt, change the shot type and camera move, choose who appears

32
00:02:12.994 --> 00:02:15.563
in the shot, and add lines of dialogue.

33
00:02:17.347 --> 00:02:18.359
Not quite right?

34
00:02:18.359 --> 00:02:22.809
Describe the change you want, and the AI rewrites the shot's prompts.

35
00:02:28.231 --> 00:02:32.131
You see exactly what would change, and decide whether to apply it.

36
00:02:34.247 --> 00:02:35.997
That's the whole story pipeline.

37
00:02:35.997 --> 00:02:40.843
In the next tutorial, we'll create reference images for the ingredients, and compose

38
00:02:40.843 --> 00:02:42.285
a keyframe for this shot.
